# Appendix B, part 1: the directory, the files, the format and the keys.
.h2 The settings directory [appcfg-dir]
.ix Settings directory
.ix .strata, directory
.ix STRATA_HOME
.ix Home directory
.ix Directory of the user
.ix Permissions of the settings
The settings are kept in one directory.  Its place is chosen in this order.
.table 6 54
Step | Rule
1 | If the environment variable {c:STRATA_HOME} is set and is not empty, the
 | directory is exactly that value.  No {c:.strata} is added to it.
2 | Otherwise the directory {c:.strata} in the home directory of the user
 | (the value of {c:HOME}, or the home directory that the system has for
 | the user when {c:HOME} is not set).
3 | If there is no home directory at all, there is no settings directory:
 | the settings are the defaults, and nothing can be saved.  A save then
 | fails with the message "There is no home directory".
.end
The Document Manager and the Editor use the same directory, for {c:config}
and for the font list.  The Printer and the Viewer read no settings.
.note {c:STRATA_HOME} is meant for people who keep several independent sets
  of settings, and for trying settings without touching your own.  When it
  is set, the older file described in {x:appcfg-legacy} is not read.
.h3 What is checked about the directory [appcfg-dir-checks]
.ix Directory checks
.ix Ownership of the settings
.ix Link as settings directory
The settings directory is private: when it is made, only you can use it.  A
directory that is a link, that is not a directory, or that belongs to another
user is not used.  Reading it gives the defaults, and a save fails with the
message "NAME is not a directory of yours".  The effect is that the settings
of one user can never be read from, or written to, a directory that another
user controls.  A directory that is a link to your own real directory is
refused too; make {c:STRATA_HOME} name the real directory instead.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: before any file in the directory is read or written the
program makes these checks, in this order.
.table 22 38
Test | Result
The name does not exist | Not an error when reading: the defaults are
 | used (see {n:appcfg-legacy}).  When saving, the directory is made and
 | closed to everybody but its owner (mode 0700).
The name is a link, or a file that | The directory cannot be used.  Reading
 is not a directory | gives the defaults; saving fails with "NAME is not a
 | directory of yours".  A link is not followed.
The directory belongs to another | The same as the line above.
 user |
The directory has a mode other | The mode is changed to 0700 and the program
 than 0700 | goes on, with no message.  If the change fails the
 | directory cannot be used.
.end
.endif
.h3 What is checked about a file [appcfg-file-checks]
.ix File checks, settings
A settings file that cannot be opened, that belongs to another user or that is
not an ordinary file is not read: the defaults are used instead.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: each file in the directory is checked once more when it is
opened.
.table 24 36
Test | Result
The file cannot be opened | For {c:config}: the defaults are used (after
 | the search for the older file, see {n:appcfg-legacy}).  For {c:fonts}:
 | the default list.
The file belongs to another user | Not read.  The defaults are used.  The
 | older file is not read either.
The file is not an ordinary file | Not read ({c:fonts} only).
The mode is not 0600 | The mode is set to 0600 and the file is read.
.end
.endif
.caution No message states that a settings file was refused.  If your
  settings are ignored, look at the owner and
  the mode of the directory and of the files with {c:ls -ld} on the
  directory and on the files in it; both must belong to you.  A file made by
  {c:root} (for example by running the Document Manager with {c:sudo}) in
  your directory makes the settings ignored until it is removed or given
  back with {c:chown}.
.h2 The files in the directory [appcfg-files]
.ix Files of the settings directory
.ix config, file
.ix fonts, file
.table 12 8 39
File | Written by | Content
config | Document Manager, Editor | The settings of the user, one line to a key
 | | (see {n:appcfg-format}).
fonts | Document Manager | The list of fonts for printing (see
 | | {n:appcfg-fonts}).
dictionary | Document Manager, Editor | The personal word list of the spell
 | | check, one word to a line ({n:spell}).  It is not a setting.
log | Document Manager | A directory that holds the log of each run of Strata
 | | (see {n:appcfg-log}).
.end
These are all the files and directories that stay in the directory.  The other
programs of Strata make no file there, and neither do the Macro and Library
commands of the Editor: macro sets and libraries are files that you name, in
a directory that you choose (see {n:appcfg-search-macro}).  The Typeface list
of Global,
Typeface is a part of the document, not of the user's settings (see
{n:appcfg-notin}).
.note Your settings are not written in the course of a normal session of
  editing.  {c:config} is written only when the Setup panel of the Document
  Manager is accepted or when the Options panel of the Editor is accepted,
  and, for one line only, when you have changed the height of the window of
  the Editor or of the Document Manager ({n:appcfg-key-rows}); {c:fonts} is
  written only when a font panel of the Document Manager is accepted.  The log
  of a run is the one thing that is made on every start of the Document
  Manager (see {n:appcfg-log}).
.h2 The log of a run [appcfg-log]
.ix Log of a run
.ix Log files
.ix Problem report, what to send
.ix run-DATE-TIME.log
.ix current.log
Every time the Document Manager is started, Strata makes a log of that run: a
text file in which the Document Manager, the Editor, the Printer and the
Viewer write what they did and what went wrong, in the order in which it
happened.  You need it when something does not work and the screen shows too
little, and the person who helps you asks for it.
.table 17 43
What | Where
The directory | The directory {c:log} of the settings directory (see
 | {n:appcfg-dir}), for example {c:~/.strata/log}.  It is made when it is
 | needed and closed to everybody but you, like the directory above it.
The file of a run | {c:run-DATE-TIME.log}: the date and the time at which the
 | run started, for example {c:run-20261114-205227.log}.
The newest run | {c:current.log} in the same directory always names the log of
 | the newest run, so that you need not look for it.
How many | The ten newest logs are kept.  When a run starts, the older logs
 | are deleted.
.end
A line of the log has this form:
.rows 20 50
{c:TIME LEVEL NAME[NUMBER]: FILE:LINE: MESSAGE} | The time with the thousandths of
 | a second; the LEVEL; the part of Strata that wrote the line and its
 | process number; the place in the program; and the message.
.end
The LEVEL is {c:FATAL} (the program cannot go on and ends), {c:ERROR}
(something failed), {c:WARN} (something was left out or could not be shown) or,
in a few more detailed lines, {c:NOTE}.  The message shows what failed, with the
name of the file or the value that it was about, and why.  The place at the
front of the message shows a developer where in the program the line was
written; it needs no explanation for you.
When the Viewer could not show something in a preview, or failed, the message
line of the Editor reports this and, because the run has a log, ends with the
words
"details in the log of the run": the log has the full text, with the page and
the place.
To read a log, open it in any editor or viewer of plain text, or show the end
of it with {c:tail}.  To report a problem, send the log of the run in which it
happened (the newest, {c:current.log}, if you report at once) together with a
sentence about what you did.  The log holds the names of your documents and of
the files that you export; read it before you send it, and cut what you do
not want to show.
.note A message that the Editor shows on its own screen (for example a
  failed export) is also in the log, with the reason.  The log is the place
  to look when the message on the screen was too short.
.h2 The file config [appcfg-format]
.ix config, format
.ix key=value lines
.ix Settings file format
.ix Autosave setting
The file is plain text.  Each line has the form {c:key=value}.  Every time the
file is saved the program writes these lines, in this order.
- Always: {c:workdir}, {c:stred}, {c:help}, {c:autosave}, {c:autosave_seconds},
  {c:expanded_status}, {c:onscreen_help}, {c:show_marks} and {c:show_dots}.
- When the value is not empty: {c:initial_macros}, {c:initial_library},
  {c:pdf_dir}, {c:pdf_viewer} and {c:spell_language}.
- When the value is not the default: {c:backups} (not 3), {c:undo_levels} (not
  100), {c:x11_rows} (not 50), {c:terminal_rows} (set), {c:terminal_charset}
  (not auto), {c:default_paper} (A4), {c:latex_pictures} (keep),
  {c:latex_positions} (yes) and {c:latex_markers} (no).
- {c:print_to}, and {c:print_queue} when it is not empty; a file of an older
  version keeps {c:printer} instead until Setup is accepted
  ({n:appcfg-key-printer}).
- The color lines that are not the default ({n:appcfg-key-colors}).
A program that keeps the height of a window changes only the line of that
height ({n:appcfg-key-rows}) and leaves every other line as it is.
.code
workdir=/home/ann/docs
stred=
help=
autosave=yes
autosave_seconds=120
expanded_status=no
onscreen_help=yes
show_marks=yes
show_dots=no
backups=5
print_to=printer
print_queue=laser
spell_language=de_DE
.end
.h3 How the file is read [appcfg-format-read]
.ix Reading the settings
.ix Unknown key
.ix Invalid value, settings
The reader is forgiving, and it is also simple.  These are all the rules.
.table 22 38
Rule | Effect
The line is split at the first {c:=} | The text before it is the key, all the
 | text after it is the value, kept as it is, spaces included.
A line with no {c:=} | Ignored.  There are no comments in the file: a line
 | that begins with a number sign has no {c:=} and so is ignored, but a
 | comment that contains {c:=} is read as a key and ignored only if the
 | key is not known.
Spaces | Not removed anywhere.  The key {c:" autosave"} is not the key
 | {c:autosave}, and the value {c:"no "} is not {c:no}.
Unknown key | Ignored, with no message.
Repeated key | The last one wins.
A missing key | The default of the key stays (see the table below).
Text values | The keys {c:workdir}, {c:stred}, {c:help}, {c:printer} and
 | {c:spell_language}.  Copied as they are: an empty value is an empty
 | setting.
Older names | The key {c:msed} is read as {c:stred} (its older name) and is
 | never written.  The key {c:backup} is described in
 | {x:appcfg-key-backups}.
Yes-or-no values | See {x:appcfg-bool}.
Number values | See {x:appcfg-int}.
.end
.h3 Yes-or-no values [appcfg-bool]
.ix Yes or no value
.ix Boolean setting
For the keys {c:autosave}, {c:expanded_status}, {c:onscreen_help},
{c:show_marks}, {c:show_dots}, {c:latex_positions} and
{c:latex_markers} the value is read like this: the
words {c:no} and {c:0} (in lower case, exactly) mean No; every other text
means Yes, and so does an empty value.  So {c:No}, {c:NO}, {c:off}, {c:false}
and {c:maybe} all mean Yes.  The program itself writes only {c:yes} and
{c:no}.  Write {c:no} in lower case when you edit the file by hand.
.note The Yes or No fields of the panels differ: they accept {c:yes} and
  {c:no} in either case (the Autosave field of
  Setup is a choice of Yes and No; see {n:appcfg-setup}).
.h3 Number values [appcfg-int]
.ix Number setting
.ix Autosave interval, range
The keys {c:autosave_seconds}, {c:backups}, {c:x11_rows}, {c:terminal_rows} and
{c:undo_levels} are numbers.  A value that is refused leaves the setting as it
was, so the default stays.  The rules differ by key.
.table 19 41
Key | Rule
{c:autosave_seconds} | Leading spaces are skipped and the digits are read until
 | the first other character, so {c:90 seconds} is 90.  A result outside 1 to
 | 86400 is refused: 0, a negative number, a text that is not a number and a
 | number over a day.
{c:backups} | Only digits, nine at most: {c:5 } and {c:5x} are refused.  A number
 | above 99 is made 99.
{c:undo_levels} | The first character must be a digit; the digits are read until
 | the first other character.  A number above 500 is made 500.
{c:x11_rows}, {c:terminal_rows} | A number above 0, read like {c:autosave_seconds}.  A
 | number below 20 is made 20 and one above 200 is made 200
 | ({n:appcfg-key-rows}).
.end
The programs write the rows in the same limits.
.h2 The keys of config [appcfg-keys]
.ix Keys of config
.ix Setting keys
.ix Defaults of the settings
This table lists every key.  Where the value is set and which program acts on
it is described in the sections that follow it.
.table 18 9 9 20
Key | Values | Default | Read by and effect
workdir | a directory | empty | Document Manager: the initial Working
 | | | Directory of the panels.
{c:initial_macros} | a file | empty | Editor: the macro set loaded at start.
{c:initial_library} | a file | empty | Editor: the library loaded at start.
stred | a file | empty | Document Manager: the Editor to start.
{c:help} | a file | empty | Document Manager: the Help File of Setup, given to
 | | | the Editor with {c:-H} ({n:str-help-search}).
autosave | yes, no | yes | Editor: save a changed document now and then.
{c:autosave_seconds} | 1 to 86400 | 120 | Editor: the interval of the autosave.
{c:expanded_status} | yes, no | no | Editor: Expanded Status Line?
{c:onscreen_help} | yes, no | yes | Editor: Display On-Screen Help?
{c:show_marks} | yes, no | yes | Editor: Display Tab & End-of-Line Characters?
{c:show_dots} | yes, no | no | Editor: Display Dots for Spaces?
backups | 0 to 99 | 3 | Editor, Document Manager: Backups Kept.
{c:print_to} | printer, file, viewer | file | Document Manager, Editor: Print To of
 | | | Setup, where a print goes by default.
{c:print_queue} | a queue name | empty | Document Manager, Editor: Printer Queue of
 | | | Setup; empty is the default queue of the system.
{c:print_last_printer} | a queue name, File only, PDF viewer | empty | Editor: the
 | | | Printer of the last print; the Print panel starts with it.
{c:print_last_sides} | default, one, two | empty | Editor: Print Double Sided? of the
 | | | last print ({c:two} is Yes, {c:one} is No).
{c:print_last_sort} | default, yes, no | empty | Editor: Sort Output Pages? of the
 | | | last print.
{c:print_last_bw} | yes, no | empty | Editor: Print in Black and White? of the
 | | | last print.
{c:pdf_dir} | a directory | empty | Document Manager, Editor: PDF Directory of
 | | | Setup; empty is the directory of the document.
{c:pdf_viewer} | a full path | empty | Document Manager, Editor: PDF Viewer of
 | | | Setup, for PDF viewer.
{c:spell_language} | a code such as en_US | empty (en_US) | Document Manager,
 | | | Editor: the Dictionary Language of the spell check.
{c:x11_rows} | 20 to 200 | empty (50) | Document Manager, Editor: the rows of the
 | | | window of the X11 version.
{c:terminal_rows} | 20 to 200 | empty (all) | Editor: the most rows of the terminal
 | | | version.
{c:terminal_charset} | {c:auto}, {c:ascii}, {c:vt100}, {c:utf8} | empty (vt100) | Document Manager, Editor:
 | | | how the terminal version draws its screen.
{c:default_paper} | letter, a4 | empty (letter) | Document Manager, Editor: the paper
 | | | of a document that is made new, without a template.
{c:undo_levels} | 0 to 500 | empty (100) | Editor: the operations that Undo keeps;
 | | | 0 turns Undo off ({n:edit-b-undo}).
{c:latex_pictures} | copy, keep | copy | Editor: Export a LaTeX File copies the
 | | | pictures, or names them in place.
{c:latex_positions} | yes, no | no | Editor: Export a LaTeX File keeps the
 | | | position comments.
{c:latex_markers} | yes, no | yes | Printer: a LaTeX marker is printed as its
 | | | words when the value is no.
x11_color_KIND_fg, | a color | empty | Document Manager, Editor: the colors
 x11_color_KIND_bg | | | of the X11 version ({n:appcfg-key-colors}).
terminal_color_KIND_fg, | a color | empty | Document Manager, Editor: the
 terminal_color_KIND_bg | | | colors of the terminal version.
.end
.h3 print_to and print_queue [appcfg-key-printer]
.ix print_to, setting
.ix print_queue, setting
.ix printer, setting
.ix Print To, setting
Values of {c:print_to}: printer, file or viewer (the older word open is read
as viewer).  Default: no line, which is file.
Values of {c:print_queue}: the name of a print queue, or empty.  Default: empty.
Print To and Printer Queue of the Setup panel.  They give the first value of
the field Printer in the Print panels of the Document Manager and of the
Editor, and where a print that is made without a panel sends the PDF when
no printer is named: file is File only (make the PDF and send nothing), viewer is
PDF viewer, and printer is the queue in {c:print_queue}, or the default queue
of the system when {c:print_queue} is empty.  When the system has no print
queue,
printer acts as File only and a message reports this.  The line {c:print_queue}
is
written only when it is not empty.
A file of an earlier version has the single key {c:printer}: a queue name,
File only, Open to print (the old name of PDF viewer), or empty.  It keeps its
meaning while the file has no
line {c:print_to}: empty or File only is file, Open to print is viewer, and any
other
text is printer with that queue.  Accepting Setup writes {c:print_to} and
{c:print_queue} and takes the line printer out.
.h3 pdf_dir [appcfg-key-pdf-dir]
.ix pdf_dir, setting
.ix PDF Directory, setting
Values: a full path, a path that starts with {c:~/}, or empty.  Default: empty,
which is the directory of the document.  The PDF Directory of the Setup
panel.  The Print panels offer {c:NAME.pdf} ({c:NAME-draft.pdf} for a Draft) in
this
directory, and so does a print that is made without a panel when no output
file is named.  A relative
path is refused by Setup and is not used when it is found in the file; a
directory that does not exist or cannot be written is not used either, and
a message reports this and the directory of the document is used.  The line is
written only when it is not empty.
.h3 pdf_viewer [appcfg-key-pdf-viewer]
.ix pdf_viewer, setting
.ix PDF, program that shows it
Values: the full path of a program, or empty.  Default: empty.  The program
that the Print panels start when the Printer field is PDF viewer: it is run
by its full path with execv, is given the full path of the PDF as its only
argument, is started without a shell, and is not waited for.  The environment
is passed on, but for {c:LD_PRELOAD} and {c:LD_AUDIT}.  The value starts with a slash
and has only letters, digits and the characters . _ + - /: no space, no
quote, no .. and no relative path.  A name such as evince is refused, and a
name or a relative path in an older settings file is not run: it is treated
as not given.  {c:PATH} is never searched.  The program must be an executable
file that other users cannot change, in a directory that others cannot write
(see {n:str-setup-fields}).  Empty means the first of evince, okular, atril,
zathura, qpdfview, mupdf, xpdf and epdfview that is in {c:/usr/bin},
{c:/usr/local/bin} or {c:/bin}.  The field PDF Viewer of Setup shows and changes it;
an empty field is saved as nothing.  A word processor is refused by Setup and is not used when it
is found in the file: the Editor then behaves as if no program was found, and
the PDF is saved.  The line is ignored when the settings file can be written
by other users, and its log reports this.  The line is written only
when it is not empty.
.h3 spell_language [appcfg-key-spell]
.ix spell_language, setting
.ix Dictionary language, setting
Values: a language code made of letters, digits and the signs _ - and @ (such
as en_US or de_DE), or empty.  Default: empty, which is en_US.  The
Dictionary Language of the Setup panel, used by the spell check.  The
line is written only when the language is not en_US.
.h3 x11_rows and terminal_rows [appcfg-key-rows]
.ix x11_rows, setting
.ix terminal_rows, setting
.ix Window height, saved
.ix Height of the window, saved
Values: a whole number of rows from 20 to 200, or no line.  Default: no line.
A program never writes a number outside the limits: a number below 20 is
written as 20, and a number above the most is written as the most (200, and
for the X11 version also not more than the rows of the screen).  Setup does
the same with a number that you type.
The rows of the window of the X11 version ({c:x11_rows}) and the rows of the
terminal version ({c:terminal_rows}) are two settings, because the two
versions are used on screens of different sizes.  Both are shown in the
Setup panel (Window Rows, X11 Version and Window Rows, Terminal Version).
.table 17 43
Key | What it does
{c:x11_rows} | The height of the window of the X11 version, in rows, for the
 | Editor and for the Document Manager.  No line, or 50, means 50 rows.  The
 | window is never taller than the screen.  When you change the height of the
 | window with the window manager, the new height is kept after a second
 | without a further change, and when the program ends at the latest; the
 | next window opens with it.  The width of the window is not kept: it is
 | always 80 columns.  The line is written only when the height is not 50.
{c:terminal_rows} | The most rows that the Editor of the terminal version uses.  No
 | line means all the rows of the terminal; a terminal that has fewer rows
 | is used as it is, and the Editor always has at least 20 rows.  The
 | program never changes the size of the window of the terminal.  When you
 | have changed the height of the terminal while the Editor ran, the Editor
 | keeps the new height in this line at the end of the run; a run in which the height
 | stayed as it was changes nothing.
.end
.note A user who never changes the height of the terminal never gets a
  {c:terminal_rows} line, and the Editor then uses all the rows of the
  terminal.  A line that you or Setup wrote makes the Editor use at most that
  many rows at its next start, even in a taller terminal; make the terminal
  taller while the Editor runs to use the room again.
.h3 terminal_charset [appcfg-key-charset]
.ix terminal_charset, setting
.ix ASCII characters, setting
Values: {c:auto}, {c:ascii}, {c:vt100} or {c:utf8}; no line means {c:vt100}, the
default.  The Terminal Characters field of the Setup panel, whose choices are
Automatic, ASCII, VT100 and UTF-8 in that order.  {c:vt100} makes the terminal
version of the Editor and of the Document Manager draw the lines with the line
characters of the VT100 set, writing ASCII characters only; {c:ascii} makes
them draw ASCII characters that look like the shapes; {c:utf8} makes them draw
the shapes of the character set as UTF-8; {c:auto} checks the terminal at the
start and chooses ({n:appterm-charset}).  An unknown value is read as no line,
so it means {c:vt100}.
The variable {c:STRATA_ASCII} (1 or 0) overrules the setting.  Setup writes the
line with the value that was chosen, so that Automatic is saved as {c:auto}: a
missing line is VT100.  The X11 version ignores it.
.h3 default_paper [appcfg-key-paper]
.ix default_paper, setting
.ix Paper, new documents
Values: {c:letter} or {c:a4}; no line means {c:letter}.  The field Paper for New
Documents of the Setup panel.  A document that is made new, in the Document
Manager or in the Editor, and does not come from a template gets this paper:
the Paper Size of Global, Page.  A template keeps its own paper, and a
document that exists keeps the paper that it has.  The line is written only
when the value is {c:a4}; another word is ignored.
.h3 The color keys [appcfg-key-colors]
.ix Colors, setting
.ix x11_color, setting
.ix terminal_color, setting
.ix Screen colors, settings
Values: a color, or empty.  Default: empty, which is the color that the
programs are delivered with.  There are two keys for every kind of text, one
for the foreground and one for the background, for each of the two versions:
the key is the version ({c:x11} or {c:terminal}), then _color_, then the kind, then _fg
or _bg, as in {c:x11_color_normal_fg} or {c:terminal_color_status_bg}.  The
two versions have separate keys so that a terminal of 16 colors and a window
of millions can each have their own.  A color is a name of the VGA palette
(black, blue, green, cyan, red, magenta, brown, light-grey, dark-grey,
light-blue, light-green, light-cyan, light-red, light-magenta, yellow, white;
capitals, spaces and hyphens do not matter) or {c:#rrggbb}, six
digits or letters a to f.  The Colors panel of Setup writes them ({n:str-colours}).
.table 15 15 29
Kind | Default | Where it is used
normal | yellow on blue | Normal text.
bold | white on blue | Bold text.
italic | light-green on blue | Italic text.
underline | yellow on blue | Underlined text.
marker | light-cyan on blue | Markers.
dim | light-grey on blue | Dim text, the ruler.
highlight | black on cyan | The highlight.
{c:print_error} | light-red on blue | Print Error markers.
keycap | black on light-grey | Keycaps.
status | black on light-grey | The status line.
message | black on light-grey | The message line.
keys | black on light-grey | The function key labels.
menu | black on light-grey | Menus.
panel | black on light-grey | Panels.
selected | white on black | The selected entry.
cursor | black on #ffb000 | The cursor; only the background key counts.
.end
A line is written only for a color that is not the default.  A bad value (a
word that is not a color, or #rrggbb with the wrong number of digits) is
reported in the log of the run, and the default color is used; the next time
that Setup writes the file the line is gone.  A key for a kind that does not
exist is ignored, as every unknown key is.  A program that is started with
the option {c:--default-colors}, or with the variable {c:STRATA_DEFAULT_COLORS}
set to a value that is not 0, ignores all of these lines.
.h3 latex_pictures [appcfg-key-latex-pictures]
.ix latex_pictures, setting
.ix Export a LaTeX File, pictures
Values: {c:copy} or {c:keep}.  Default: {c:copy}.  The word {c:keep} means
keep; every other text means copy.  With copy, Export a LaTeX File
({n:file-export-latex}) copies each picture of the document into the directory
beside the file and names it there; with keep the file names each picture
where it is.  A picture of PostScript is copied in both cases.  The Setup
panel has no field for it: edit the file.  The line is written only when the
value is keep.
.h3 latex_positions [appcfg-key-latex-positions]
.ix latex_positions, setting
.ix Export a LaTeX File, position comments
Values: yes or no.  Default: no.  With yes, Export a LaTeX File keeps before
each line of the file a comment that says which line of the document it came
from; with no the comments are left out.  The Setup panel has no field for
it: edit the file.  The line is written only when the value is yes.
.h3 latex_markers [appcfg-key-latex-markers]
.ix latex_markers, setting
.ix Allow LaTeX Markers
.ix LaTeX markers, not allowed
Values: yes or no.  Default: yes.  With yes, every print of a document (Print,
Preview and Export a LaTeX File) writes the text of its LaTeX markers
({n:marker-latex}) into the page; with no the text is not written, each
marker is printed as the words LaTeX instructions, and the Error Log has a
warning for each one.  Use no to print a document of someone you do not know
without the instructions that it holds.  The Setup panel has no field for it:
edit the file.  The line is written only when the value is no.
.h3 initial_macros and initial_library [appcfg-key-initial]
.ix initial_macros
.ix initial_library
.ix Initial Macro Set, setting
.ix Initial Library, setting
Values: the name of a file, or empty.  Default: empty, which is saved as no
line.  The Editor reads the two keys at start and loads the macro set and the
library before the first key is read.  A name that begins with a slash
is the file; any other name is looked for in the working directory of the
Editor, then in the directory of the settings; a name with no period gets
{c:.mac} or {c:.lib}.  They are set by the fields Initial Macro Set and Initial
Library of Setup ({n:str-setup-fields}).  A file that cannot be used is a
message and a line of the log; the Editor starts anyway.

.h3 workdir [appcfg-key-workdir]
.ix workdir
.ix Initial Working Directory
.ix Working directory, initial
Values: the name of a directory, or empty.  Default: empty.
.table 17 43
Where | What happens
Document Manager at start | If the value names a directory that exists, it
 | is the Working Directory that the Edit / Preview / Print Document
 | panel shows.  If it is empty, or names something that
 | is not a directory, the current directory of the shell is used.
Setup panel | The field Initial Working Directory.  Accepting Setup with a
 | name that is not an existing directory is refused (see
 | {n:appcfg-setup}); an empty field is accepted.
Editor | Not used.  The Editor takes its working directory from the document
 | that it has open or from its current directory.
.end
.caution The field of Setup is never empty when it is first shown: if the
  key is empty, the field shows the current directory.  Accepting Setup
  without changing that field therefore saves the current directory as
  {c:workdir}, and from then on it is no longer empty.  Clear the field
  (with {k:Backspace}) to keep the file empty.
.h3 The key for the Editor [appcfg-key-stred]
.ix Editor program, setting
Values: the name of a program file, or empty.  Default: empty.  The key is
named {c:stred}.  The Document Manager starts this program for Edit / Preview
/ Print Document.
The search
that gives the value its meaning, and the reason why the value is usually
empty, are in {x:str-find-editor}.  In short, the key is used only when it
names a file that may be run; otherwise the Document Manager looks at the
Editor's file in its own
directory, then in the system directories.  When Setup is accepted the path is saved only
if it differs from the one that the search finds by itself, so that a program
that was found is not fixed in the file; a file made by Setup
therefore has an empty value unless you typed another path.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the environment variable {c:STRED} is looked at after the key
and
before the Editor's file in the directory of the Document Manager.
.endif
.h3 autosave [appcfg-key-autosave]
.ix Autosave
.ix autosave
.ix Autosave, Yes or No
Values: {c:yes} or {c:no} (see {n:appcfg-bool}).  Default: yes.  The Editor
reads it at start.  With Autosave Yes a document that has unsaved changes
and has a name is saved under that name as soon as the autosave interval has
passed since its first unsaved change.  No question is put.  A document with no
name is not saved.  The save is the same as the Save command, but it
makes no numbered backup.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the option {c:--autosave no} of the Editor
({n:appcmd-stred-opt}) turns autosave off for one run; {c:--autosave yes}
does not turn it on if the file says no.
.endif
.h3 autosave_seconds [appcfg-key-seconds]
.ix autosave_seconds
.ix Autosave interval
.ix Save interval
Values: a whole number from 1 to 86400.  Default: 120.  The interval of the
autosave in seconds; see {x:appcfg-int} for the way a bad value is treated.
The Document Manager
starts the Editor with both values set from its own Setup, so a value that
was accepted in Setup is used by the Editor even before it is read from
the file.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the option {c:--autosave-seconds N} of the Editor replaces it
for one run if N is in the same range, and is ignored otherwise.
.endif
.h3 expanded_status [appcfg-key-expanded]
.ix expanded_status
.ix Expanded Status Line
Values: yes or no.  Default: no.  Read by the Editor at start; it is the
setting of Global, Options, Expanded Status Line?.  Yes shows the longer
status line (see the chapter on the screen).
.h3 onscreen_help [appcfg-key-help-line]
.ix onscreen_help
.ix On-Screen Help
.ix Key help lines
Values: yes or no.  Default: yes.  Read by the Editor at start; it is the
setting of Global, Options, Display On-Screen Help?.  Yes shows the two lines
of key reminders at the bottom of the Editor screen.
.h3 show_marks [appcfg-key-marks]
.ix show_marks
.ix Tab and end-of-line characters
Values: yes or no.  Default: yes.  Read by the Editor at start; it is the
setting of Global, Options, Display Tab & End-of-Line Characters?.  Yes
shows the tab and end-of-line signs on the screen.
.h3 show_dots [appcfg-key-dots]
.ix show_dots
.ix Dots for spaces
Values: yes or no.  Default: no.  Read by the Editor at start; it is the
setting of Global, Options, Display Dots for Spaces?.  Yes shows a dot for
every space on the screen.
.h3 backups [appcfg-key-backups]
.ix backups
.ix Backups Kept, setting
.ix .BAK
Values: a whole number from 0 to 99.  Default: 3, which is saved as no line.
It is read by the Editor at start and set by the field Backups Kept of Setup
in the Document Manager and of Global, Options in the Editor.  It is the
number of numbered backups, such as {c:.backup/report.bak01}, that a Save keeps
of the file that it replaces; 0 makes none.  Lowering it shows a box first
({n:file-backup-option}).  A number above 99 is made 99.  A settings
file that has the old key {c:backup} (yes or no) is read as if it was not
there: the first versions wrote it, with its default no, in every file, so
that a no was never a choice.  The default of 3 stays; only the key
{c:backups} sets another number, and {c:backups=0} stays 0.  The key
{c:backup} is not written any more.
